.TH __CHTTPSVR_DESTROY 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
__chttpsvr_destroy \- free an HTTP server
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <chttpserver.h>

void __chttpsvr_destroy(chttpsvr srv);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Releases this server's reference to the shared facil.io engine (shared
with chttpclient's async engine), then drains all in-flight requests
for this server (up to 30 seconds), then frees all routers, routes,
middleware nodes, the server's owned ctpool, and the server struct
itself.

If this was the last reference to the shared engine (from either this
server or any other chttpsvr instance, or from chttpclient's async
engine), the engine's actual teardown happens asynchronously on a
separate thread and is NOT guaranteed complete by the time this
function returns.  Call chttpsvr_engine_wait() afterward for a
synchronous guarantee (e.g. before reusing the port this server was
listening on, or before process exit).

Also calls clog_close on the server's own logger (the internal
stderr/FATAL-only logger, or the logger derived from the cl passed to
create_chttpsvr_mp / create_chttpsvr).  This never closes a
caller-supplied cl directly, since the server only ever stores a
derived copy of it.

For clean multi-server teardown, call chttpsvr_stop on all servers
first to close their listeners, then call __chttpsvr_destroy on each
handle in any order.

Does not set the caller's pointer to NULL; use the chttpsvr_destroy
macro for that.

Safe to call with NULL.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_destroy (3),
.BR chttpsvr_stop (3),
.BR chttpsvr_engine_wait (3)
